WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.033
Describing the Essence of Value Creation

2
00:00:05.033 --> 00:00:07.699
Welcome back to this session on value creation.

3
00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:12.766
What we’re going to do is look at an implied example here, to help us better understand that.

4
00:00:12.766 --> 00:00:18.732
Now remember, we can use value creation at a personal level,

5
00:00:18.733 --> 00:00:22.733
as well as a professional level.

6
00:00:22.733 --> 00:00:27.733
Now, we looked at a very rudimentary type of personal example,

7
00:00:27.733 --> 00:00:31.866
when we applied it to the newspaper in kind of a got a joking way.

8
00:00:31.866 --> 00:00:36.432
With the dog, eating the newspaper thereby restraining us

9
00:00:36.433 --> 00:00:39.666
from having an opportunity to get a job.

10
00:00:39.666 --> 00:00:42.666
Now what we’re going to do is look it at a professional level.

11
00:00:42.666 --> 00:00:48.832
In this case we’re going to use a little product example. To make it very clear and succinct.

12
00:00:48.833 --> 00:00:53.199
Essentially, let's imagine that we've got a product out there.

13
00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:59.900
A piece of hardware. Could be an electronic device. We’ve talked about that before.

14
00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:01.966
And it's full of CTQ’s.

15
00:01:01.966 --> 00:01:07.932
So, that has got critical to quality characteristic 1 to critical to quality characteristic N.

16
00:01:07.933 --> 00:01:11.666
That makes up that product.

17
00:01:11.666 --> 00:01:15.099
Everything from the performance of it at the system level,

18
00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:19.166
down to how well all the little resistors and diodes and everything else works.

19
00:01:19.166 --> 00:01:22.099
Those are all critical to quality.

20
00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:27.066
Well, it's a pretty simple little way to go. We’re going to pick a CTQ.

21
00:01:27.066 --> 00:01:31.366
We’re going to call it CTQ sub Y.

22
00:01:31.366 --> 00:01:36.232
Whatever that one could be, the first one, the fourth one, the fifth one. Who cares?

23
00:01:36.233 --> 00:01:39.833
So, as we start to look at this,

24
00:01:39.833 --> 00:01:45.299
we recognize again the simple steps.

25
00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:50.933
We have to recognize the value need. Now, what's the value need?

26
00:01:50.933 --> 00:01:55.766
That is the performance

27
00:01:55.766 --> 00:02:02.799
of this CTQ. That's the utilitarian need.

28
00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:08.366
So, that is in fact

29
00:02:08.366 --> 00:02:12.966
the value need that we have is the performance of this CTQ.

30
00:02:12.966 --> 00:02:18.732
So, if we look on a scale of performance. This is the scale of performance.

31
00:02:18.733 --> 00:02:22.633
This might be how far out you can talk on that walkie-talkie.

32
00:02:22.633 --> 00:02:27.833
See. That could be, you know, 500 feet, 600, 700, blah, blah, blah, blah.

33
00:02:27.833 --> 00:02:30.833
Up here to a mile away.

34
00:02:30.833 --> 00:02:34.333
So, what the basic situation is,

35
00:02:34.333 --> 00:02:38.633
is that now we've gotta define that change opportunity.

36
00:02:38.633 --> 00:02:42.466
We've got to take it maybe from here to there.

37
00:02:42.466 --> 00:02:47.299
From an is condition to some ought condition, on that scale.

38
00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:52.333
That's what we need to go do. So, that's the value need.

39
00:02:52.333 --> 00:02:57.599
Performance of CTQ Y, which is a utilitarian need.

40
00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:01.600
Which is a functional need. Remember that? Form, fit and function. This is function.

41
00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:08.200
And we recognize that this is the current, and this is what ought to be.

42
00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:11.400
And we gotta drive it from there to there.

43
00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:16.566
Now, what we need to do is to define the change opportunity. That's that.

44
00:03:16.566 --> 00:03:23.332
We’ve got to translate it from a product issue into a process issue.

45
00:03:23.333 --> 00:03:29.633
In other words, we have to now start to talk about, in step two,

46
00:03:29.633 --> 00:03:33.866
defining the change opportunity to talk about yield.

47
00:03:33.866 --> 00:03:40.832
So, here's yield, 70, 80, 90, oops, 100.

48
00:03:40.833 --> 00:03:46.466
So, we might currently have a yield of 80%.

49
00:03:46.466 --> 00:03:50.332
That's the current. That's the is condition.

50
00:03:50.333 --> 00:03:56.133
We may need to take it to 90%.

51
00:03:56.133 --> 00:04:02.833
At this level of performance. See, at the ought right now, only 80% are being created.

52
00:04:02.833 --> 00:04:09.633
But we need 90% at that level. So, I translated this now, to a process problem.

53
00:04:09.633 --> 00:04:12.599
Improve the yield.

54
00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:18.000
Now, step three is to measure the existing conditions.

55
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:23.766
So, I go out there and I measure it and I confirm, yeah, 80%.

56
00:04:23.766 --> 00:04:28.132
That's the way it is right now. That's the is condition.

57
00:04:28.133 --> 00:04:32.133
There’s my goal, over at 90%.

58
00:04:32.133 --> 00:04:37.533
Now, what I gotta go do is step number four. Analyze the contributing forces.

59
00:04:37.533 --> 00:04:43.933
I gotta recognize that sitting out there, in the process.

60
00:04:43.933 --> 00:04:50.933
Recall, that I got a process here. Step one, two, three, and out here it creates my product.

61
00:04:50.933 --> 00:04:56.933
I gotta improve my yield from the baseline of 80% to 90%.

62
00:04:56.933 --> 00:04:59.933
Now, how am I going to do that?

63
00:04:59.933 --> 00:05:06.299
I gotta go out here and look at all the X’s, X1, X2, X3

64
00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:11.066
to XN, in the process. I gotta go look at all the process variables.

65
00:05:11.066 --> 00:05:16.266
These are the knobs. Remember that. These are the knobs in the process

66
00:05:16.266 --> 00:05:20.432
These are the knobs in the process that controls how good the yield is going to be.

67
00:05:20.433 --> 00:05:27.233
So, I gotta go identify these through brainstorming, and then analyze them.

68
00:05:27.233 --> 00:05:32.033
I need to look at which of those X's is driving me

69
00:05:32.033 --> 00:05:37.433
toward my 90% goal and which is restraining me from that.

70
00:05:37.433 --> 00:05:42.833
In other words, which of these is my vital few and which is the trivial many?

71
00:05:42.833 --> 00:05:47.399
Then what I gotta go do is for every single one of those.

72
00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:50.400
I’ll pick one of them with leverage.

73
00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:57.433
Remember, some of these are among the vital few and most are among the trivial many.

74
00:05:57.433 --> 00:06:02.133
I go find the vital few. That's what I got. I got the vital few drivers,

75
00:06:02.133 --> 00:06:05.066
and I got the vital few restrainers.

76
00:06:05.066 --> 00:06:11.599
Now, I’m going to take one of those X’s. The big hitter.

77
00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:18.466
And I'm going to say that its force is from here to here.

78
00:06:18.466 --> 00:06:25.299
Now, what I want to do is put an extension on that force. I want to make more force.

79
00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:29.300
So, I need to increase the force of that variable.

80
00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:34.733
Which might be training of my people, for example. They may not be well enough trained.

81
00:06:34.733 --> 00:06:38.733
So, I need to increase the training.

82
00:06:38.733 --> 00:06:45.566
See. That's one of the critical X’s.

83
00:06:45.566 --> 00:06:51.232
That is a critical to process variable.

84
00:06:51.233 --> 00:06:56.033
That is a contributory force.

85
00:06:56.033 --> 00:07:02.733
So I need to go increase it, see, from there to here. I need to make that a stronger force.

86
00:07:02.733 --> 00:07:05.966
They need to get more knowledge. If they get more knowledge.

87
00:07:05.966 --> 00:07:10.532
What it’s going to do is push the situation in a more positive direction.

88
00:07:10.533 --> 00:07:14.666
Getting me closer to my goal of 90%.

89
00:07:14.666 --> 00:07:21.499
Which ultimately gets more performing at this ought condition.

90
00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:25.500
Which satisfies the originating need.

91
00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:30.366
Now, after I improve that action setting.

92
00:07:30.366 --> 00:07:35.566
That variable that we looked at called training.

93
00:07:35.566 --> 00:07:42.666
See, I took it from there and I extended it. I improved it. I made it bigger.

94
00:07:42.666 --> 00:07:46.666
Before. After.

95
00:07:46.666 --> 00:07:53.066
Now, what I gotta do is keep that from varying. In other words, I gotta sustain the gains.

96
00:07:53.066 --> 00:07:58.066
I recognize that there's variation around these points.

97
00:07:58.066 --> 00:08:04.632
So, I want to minimize the variation, so I get consistency of application of training.

98
00:08:04.633 --> 00:08:08.466
I need to remove those variations.

99
00:08:08.466 --> 00:08:13.732
Then I need to control those input variations over time.

100
00:08:13.733 --> 00:08:19.799
I need to control those things that create the variation around training. Get rid of them.

101
00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:26.833
For example, instructor to instructor differences in philosophy in teaching style,

102
00:08:26.833 --> 00:08:30.066
causes the training to vary.

103
00:08:30.066 --> 00:08:36.732
As the training varies, the force of my people, my workforce varies.

104
00:08:36.733 --> 00:08:43.366
As it varies, I become less certain that I’m going to go from 80 to 90%.

105
00:08:43.366 --> 00:08:48.132
So, that's what we mean by control input variations.

106
00:08:48.133 --> 00:08:53.933
And then finally I look across everything that I did

107
00:08:53.933 --> 00:09:00.933
in those six steps from step 1, 2, 3 through step 6, that I did.

108
00:09:00.933 --> 00:09:06.199
Recall what those were. Recognize value needs. Define change opportunities.

109
00:09:06.200 --> 00:09:13.133
Through those first six steps. And I say, what made it successful?

110
00:09:13.133 --> 00:09:17.133
Well I standardize on those success factors.

111
00:09:17.133 --> 00:09:20.699
Try to apply it to my other processes.

112
00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:24.433
In other words, if training gotta bump on yield in this process,

113
00:09:24.433 --> 00:09:28.433
well, let me go take that and try other processes.

114
00:09:28.433 --> 00:09:31.999
If it works, standardize on it

115
00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:38.800
and then integrate that lesson learned back into new policies and procedures.

116
00:09:38.800 --> 00:09:44.466
So, that's ultimately how we move forward

117
00:09:44.466 --> 00:09:50.899
with improving a critical to quality characteristic

118
00:09:50.900 --> 00:09:55.333
which helps me satisfy a value need.

119
00:09:55.333 --> 00:09:59.633
That's what value creation is all about.

120
00:09:59.633 --> 00:10:04.433
It's a simple process consisting of eight steps.

121
00:10:04.433 --> 00:10:10.066
That's how we do it, to create value. That's what value creation is all about.

122
00:10:10.066 --> 00:10:15.066
Thank you for choosing SSMI.
